## Key content already extracted (from ar5iv HTML, cross-checked against arXiv abstract)

### Abstract (paraphrase)
Mitiq is an open-source Python package for quantum error mitigation on near-term (NISQ) quantum computers. It implements zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE), probabilistic error cancellation (PEC), and Clifford data regression (CDR), and interfaces with major quantum SDKs (Cirq, Qiskit, pyQuil, Braket).

### Section 3.3 — PEC toy example (source of Fig 5 numbers)
Circuit: `U = CNOT_{1,2} ∘ X_1 ∘ H_2`. Observable: `O = |00⟩⟨00|`. Noiseless expectation: 0.
Noise: single-qubit depolarizing p=0.1 after every gate on a density-matrix simulator.
- Unmitigated `⟨00|ρ|00⟩` = **0.0622**
- PEC-mitigated (1000 samples) `⟨00|ρ|00⟩` ≈ **0.0071**
- Improvement: "almost an order of magnitude"

### Section 2 / Section 3.1 — ZNE benchmarks (source of Fig 3-4 claims)
ZNE via unitary folding + Richardson extrapolation. Applied to benchmark circuits (Clifford RB, H₂ VQE). Claim: mitigated expectation values are closer to noiseless truth than unmitigated across benchmarks.

### Multi-backend
Cirq / Qiskit / pyQuil / Braket support advertised. Hardware runs shown on IBM Q and Rigetti devices (Fig 3).

